Let libsoup log the wire conversation in a release build.

https:// hangs here while http:// works, with the connection ESTABLISHED and
both queues empty, and curl plus a standalone libsoup program fetch the same
URL fine.  Nothing short of the actual bytes distinguishes "we never wrote a
request" from "we wrote one and the server answered nothing", and every
attempt to reason it out from the layers below has been wrong.

WEBKIT_DEBUG=Network produces nothing, because setupLogger()'s whole body sits
inside

    #if !LOG_DISABLED || !RELEASE_LOG_DISABLED

and both are disabled in a release build -- so no SoupLogger is ever attached.
soupLogPrinter is behind the same guard and its body is LOG()/RELEASE_LOG(),
which compile to nothing too.  Soup's logging does NOT need a debug build --
LoggingUnix.cpp says so in as many words ("some of our logging, such as Soup's
is available in release builds") -- it only needs a logger on the session.

So: a printer built on WTFLogAlways, which survives a release build, and an
opt-in through WEBKIT_SOUP_LOG that attaches the logger directly and skips the
log-channel machinery.  Off unless the variable is set, so a normal run is
unchanged.

Upstream: not reportable -- upstream's arrangement is deliberate; this is a
bring-up aid for a platform where the usual debug build is impractical.

--- a/Source/WebCore/platform/network/soup/SoupNetworkSession.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/platform/network/soup/SoupNetworkSession.cpp
@@ -54,6 +54,15 @@
     return storage.get();
 }
 
+// Always compiled, unlike the one below: WTFLogAlways survives a release
+// build, LOG and RELEASE_LOG do not.  Used by the WEBKIT_SOUP_LOG path in
+// setupLogger().  The direction character is libsoup's own: '>' is a request
+// header going out, '<' a response header coming back, ' ' body data.
+static void soupReleaseLogPrinter(SoupLogger*, SoupLoggerLogLevel, char direction, const char* data, gpointer)
+{
+    WTFLogAlways("[soup] %c %s", direction, data);
+}
+
 #if !LOG_DISABLED || !RELEASE_LOG_DISABLED
 inline static void soupLogPrinter(SoupLogger*, SoupLoggerLogLevel, char direction, const char* data, gpointer)
 {
@@ -154,6 +163,27 @@
 
 void SoupNetworkSession::setupLogger()
 {
+    // WEBKIT_SOUP_LOG makes libsoup log the wire conversation in a RELEASE
+    // build.  The block below is the upstream path, and it is compiled out
+    // whenever LOG_DISABLED and RELEASE_LOG_DISABLED are both set -- which is
+    // every release build, so WEBKIT_DEBUG=Network produces nothing at all.
+    // Soup's own logging does not need a debug build (LoggingUnix.cpp says so
+    // in as many words), only a SoupLogger attached to the session, so this
+    // attaches one directly and skips the log-channel machinery entirely.
+    //
+    // This exists because https:// hangs here with the connection ESTABLISHED
+    // and both queues empty, while curl and a standalone libsoup program fetch
+    // the same URL fine.  Nothing short of the actual request and response
+    // bytes distinguishes "we never wrote anything" from "we wrote it and the
+    // server said nothing".
+    if (getenv("WEBKIT_SOUP_LOG") && !soup_session_get_feature(m_soupSession.get(), SOUP_TYPE_LOGGER)) {
+        GRefPtr<SoupLogger> logger = adoptGRef(soup_logger_new(SOUP_LOGGER_LOG_BODY));
+        soup_session_add_feature(m_soupSession.get(), SOUP_SESSION_FEATURE(logger.get()));
+        soup_logger_set_printer(logger.get(), soupReleaseLogPrinter, nullptr, nullptr);
+        WTFLogAlways("[soup] logger attached to session");
+        return;
+    }
+
 #if !LOG_DISABLED || !RELEASE_LOG_DISABLED
     if (LogNetwork.state != WTFLogChannelState::On || soup_session_get_feature(m_soupSession.get(), SOUP_TYPE_LOGGER))
         return;
